  "_exec_summary": [
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000122027,
      "SummaryText": "Cove Fort is an Organic Rankine Cycle geothermal power plant. The working fluid is normal pentane. Cove Fort has two, identical Rankine Cycle processes. Each process is referred to as an Ormat Energy Converter (OEC), identified as OEC-1 and OEC-2. The OEC converts heat captured from geothermal brine to electric power. The geothermal brine is supplied by five, VFD-driven pumps at 120 psig and 310\u00b0F. Four pumps are submersible (ESP) and one is a line shaft pump (LSP). Effluent brine is reinjected in three injection wells and does not require the use of brine injection pumps.\n\nEach OEC operates at two, separate pressure levels (145 psia and 78 psia) that capture heat from the brine in shell and tube preheaters and shell and tube vaporizers. The vaporized normal pentane passes through a demister vessel, located on top of the vaporizer in each pressure level, which captures any liquid normal pentane droplets and delivers superheated vapor to high and low pressure turbines. The two turbines are connected to a single double-ended shaft generator operating at 1800 RPM. Each turbine exhaust vapor stream is directed to air-cooled condensers, where it is de-superheated and condensed. The condensed normal pentane is the then pumped back through the process in a closed loop cycle.\n\nThere are multiple auxiliary systems including a VRMU, that is used to evacuate normal pentane vapors from equipment prior to opening, and a VRU system that is used to remove non-condensable gas from the Rankine Cycle process, making it operate more efficiently. Other auxiliaries include the turbine lubrication/seal oil system, feed pump Plan 53b seal plan, Plan 52 feed pump seal plans, fire water system, and instrument air system."
    }
  ]
